  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Rounds of showers and scattered thunderstorms with \nlocalized heavy rainfall are expected through tonight.\n\nAloft, cyclonic and mainly NE'erly to N'erly flow is expected to\nimpact Western and Central NY through tonight as a mid/upper- \nlevel low wobbles SE'ward from Lake Ontario to near Long Island.\nSimultaneously and at the surface, a trough lingers over most \nof our CWA as the primary surface low wobbles NE'ward from NJ to\nnear Long Island and becomes vertically-stacked with the \naforementioned low aloft. However, a ridge accompanied by \nstabilizing subsidence will build slowly into WNY from the \nwestern Great Lakes through tonight. Isolated to scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms are expected, especially roughly along\nand east of the Genesee River and during this afternoon through\nearly evening, as the fairly-moist boundary layer undergoes \nweak to moderate destabilization via daytime heating. Showers \nand storms will be triggered by the following: low-level \nconvergence/moist ascent along the surface trough axis extending\nfrom the aforementioned low into eastern portions of our CWA \nand along surface trough axes that will accompany subtle \nshortwave disturbances embedded in the cyclonic flow aloft; \nmoist isentropic ascent releasing weak elevated CAPE in the \nweakening warm conveyor belt of the low pressure system. Periods\nof steady to heavy rain are expected as the warm conveyor belt \ntaps into abundant moisture originating over the Gulf and \nsouthern Gulf Stream. A very marginal risk of localized flash \nflooding exists through daybreak Thursday in the Eastern Lake \nOntario Region, especially Lewis County.\n\nWeak low-level CAA persists through tonight as Western and\nCentral NY remain along the northwestern flank of the \naforementioned surface low. Overnight lows in the 50's or 60's \nare expected around daybreak this morning and Thursday morning, \nrespectively. High temperatures should reach the mid 60's to \nupper 70's late this afternoon. The coolest highs are expected \nin the higher terrain of the Western Southern Tier, Wyoming \nCounty, the Finger Lakes, and Eastern Lake Ontario Region. \n\nKey Message 2...Increasingly drier conditions return for the end of \nthe work week. \n\nOn Thursday through Friday, the low pressure system at the\nsurface and aloft will exit generally NE'ward toward the\nCanadian Maritimes. Simultaneously, the aforementioned surface\nridge and the portion of the ridge aloft will continue to build\nfrom the western Great Lakes and vicinity so that by late \nFriday, the surface ridge axis should become located near Long\nIsland and Lake Champlain. While lingering low-level moisture, \nrenewed diurnal heating, and weak boundary layer destabilization\nmay support self-destructive sunshine in the form of isolated \nto scattered shower and isolated thunderstorm development \nroughly along and east of the Genesee River Valley on Thursday, \nstabilizing subsidence accompanying the building ridge will \nyield fair weather CWA-wide late Thursday evening through \nFriday. Given the projected weather pattern evolution at the \nsurface and aloft, weak low-level CAA through Thursday night is\nexpected to transition to weak low-level WAA on the synoptic \nscale by Friday. \n\nLate afternoon highs on Thursday should reach the 70's to 80F.\nConsiderable clearing of sky, lower humidity at/near the\nsurface, and weak surface winds should permit efficient\nradiational cooling and allow lows to reach mainly the 50's to\nlower 60's around daybreak Friday. River valley steam fog is\nexpected around daybreak in the Western Southern Tier, Finger\nLakes, and the Saint Lawrence and Black River Valleys. With the\ndeparture of the low aloft and its associated cool pool, high \ntemperatures should recover to seasonable levels (upper 70's to\nlower 80's) areawide by Friday.",
